WEBVTT

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:05.920
Earlier this year, we started seeing something internally and at the organizations we work with

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:15.280
that concerned us. And that's that it's becoming normal to vibe-analyze data. A broader population

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:23.440
of people can now convincingly do data analysis by pulling up a coding agent and asking a data

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:30.080
question. There was an episode of Posit's Test Set podcast earlier this year that

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:37.840
phrased this pretty hilariously. The title is, Your VP is Doing a Rogue Analysis in Cursor

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:45.200
Right Now. And in this podcast, Danielle Thomas describes how she's seeing this in her organization.

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:54.959
I think data people are kind of horrified at the idea of vibe analysis. It sets a low bar for

00:00:54.959 --> 00:01:02.799
correctness, for reproducibility. You know, these coding agents, they tend to work in temp files.

00:01:03.759 --> 00:01:08.559
The harnesses that they work in tend to delete the conversation histories after a month.

00:01:09.519 --> 00:01:15.760
And so it doesn't set these models up well to write code that will be able to

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:25.519
reproduce the same result even a month later. But at the same time, we can't just ignore this or say

00:01:26.480 --> 00:01:31.120
not to do it. People are going to reach for the technology that gives them answers.

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:37.760
And we think that data people have the skill set to serve these people with chatbots that give more

00:01:37.760 --> 00:01:45.360
correct and more reproducible answers. So if these models are going to make mistakes, if they

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:52.000
have bad habits in data analysis, how do we make data analysis agents that are trustworthy?

00:01:54.559 --> 00:02:00.879
I think there are kind of two problems that we have to speak to in order to really answer this.

00:02:01.839 --> 00:02:10.399
One is that we need the model to write the right code. And we also need the user,

00:02:11.839 --> 00:02:18.800
the user has to know how trustworthy that code is. So if it's code that the data team

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:26.160
is maintaining already, and these are sources of trusted analysis already that might underlie

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:31.119
like a Shiny app or a quarter report that exists from the data team, then that code is more

00:02:31.119 --> 00:02:40.240
trustworthy than code that the agent is sort of improvising on the spot. These are exactly the

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:48.000
problems that we're trying to solve in a new open source package called Commons. Commons is a tool

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:54.240
for data scientists to build trustworthy data agents for their collaborators. And those data

00:02:54.240 --> 00:03:01.679
agents are designed to be more correct, more reproducible, and more transparent than coding

00:03:01.679 --> 00:03:11.440
agents that are most popular. It's open source in both R and Python, and it's built on our open

00:03:11.440 --> 00:03:18.880
source AI stack with Shiny, Elmer, and Chatless. And you can use it with whatever model from

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:25.679
whatever provider that you want. I'm often asked why it is that there's a bird on the hex.

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:36.639
This is a common kingfisher. So Commons, common. One of the core ideas behind Commons is this

00:03:36.639 --> 00:03:43.520
concept of trusted code. So I mentioned earlier that the data team already has all sorts of

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:51.119
code that produces analysis that is trusted by their collaborators and within their organization.

00:03:51.919 --> 00:03:57.839
So this could be R packages, Python packages, quarter reports, Shiny apps,

00:03:58.559 --> 00:04:05.919
semantic views inside of databases like Databricks or Snowflake. And one of the

00:04:05.919 --> 00:04:10.720
questions that Commons tries to answer is what would it look like if the model could reach for

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:18.160
this code directly rather than sort of hand authoring code on its own. So instead of writing

00:04:18.160 --> 00:04:25.679
code afresh, it's just reaching for this trusted code when it's around. So in order to make that

00:04:25.679 --> 00:04:31.920
happen, Commons is doing three things. The first of them is providing a data structure that you can

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:39.279
extract all of this trusted code and metadata about what that code means and how it's interpreted

00:04:39.359 --> 00:04:47.920
in the business context into this common data structure. And once that data structure exists,

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:55.519
there's also a harness, so a set of prompts and tools that's provided to an LLM to try to give

00:04:55.519 --> 00:05:03.519
it the right context at the right time. And finally, once these agents exist and they're

00:05:03.519 --> 00:05:10.799
deployed in production, those agents will do a bunch of work live. And some of those

00:05:11.679 --> 00:05:18.160
conversations will result in the agent finding code that is trusted by the organization already

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:25.279
and invoking it. But some of the other questions will result in the agent having to improvise

00:05:25.279 --> 00:05:31.119
R or Python code. And so in those cases where the agent has to improvise code,

00:05:32.079 --> 00:05:36.559
Commons provides some tools to try to push those responses up the trust ladder,

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:42.399
allowing the data team to see what questions are being asked live

00:05:42.399 --> 00:05:46.559
and add new sources of trusted code over time.

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:56.480
So let's see this in action from the perspective of the VP doing a rogue analysis,

00:05:56.480 --> 00:06:02.640
except this time the analysis is a bit less rogue. We're looking at a regular,

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:11.279
regular chat application. We've designed Commons apps to look as much like regular coding agents

00:06:11.279 --> 00:06:18.720
as possible. So this should feel familiar to users of products like chat.com or cloud.ai.

00:06:18.959 --> 00:06:28.000
So that VP might ask, how is traffic trending for our site? So the first way that these

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:33.440
Commons agents work a little bit differently than most coding agents is that the agent will first

00:06:33.440 --> 00:06:40.000
search for a trusted calculation. And if it can find some piece of code that the data team uses

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:47.519
already, then it will invoke that directly rather than writing code on its own. And so

00:06:48.480 --> 00:06:55.119
so in this case, it's found a calculation that is trusted by the data team, and it can invoke it,

00:06:55.760 --> 00:07:00.399
and then it will write a bit of context about what it sees.

00:07:02.399 --> 00:07:07.679
The last thing you might notice is that the end of the response has this little green shield

00:07:07.679 --> 00:07:13.920
attached to the end. And if you hover over that green shield, you'll see that this answer comes

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:26.239
from a trusted calculation. This green shield is added deterministically by the Commons system

00:07:26.239 --> 00:07:32.640
rather than by the agent, because the only way that the agent accessed and analyzed data

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:43.200
is via the trusted calculation. We know that trusted code probably won't cover all of the

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:48.880
possible questions that users might have of an application like this, so it is possible for

00:07:48.880 --> 00:07:56.160
Commons agents to write custom R and Python and SQL code in order to answer questions.

00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:05.600
So the first thing that the agent will do in this sort of situation is look through the trusted

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:11.839
calculations at first, and if it can't find it, it will search through context defined by the data

00:08:11.839 --> 00:08:20.399
team about how these calculations are done in the organization. So this could be data dictionaries,

00:08:20.399 --> 00:08:27.279
markdown files, if you have them existing context layers from like Databricks or Snowflake,

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:31.279
and then it can write whatever code it needs to find an answer.

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:38.479
So in this case, it was able to make a plot with some R code and describe it to me,

00:08:38.479 --> 00:08:45.679
but it also knows how to interpret this plot because of some context that was defined by

00:08:45.679 --> 00:08:54.080
the data team. You'll notice at the end of this answer, there's a little blue citation icon,

00:08:54.080 --> 00:09:02.559
and if I hover over that, the agent has cited some piece of context that justifies its approach,

00:09:03.440 --> 00:09:10.479
and thus the response doesn't fall into the lowest trust tier, which I'll show now.

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:17.359
If I ask a question and the agent doesn't necessarily find some piece of context

00:09:17.359 --> 00:09:25.760
that justifies its approach, the end of its response will have a little yellow exclamation point,

00:09:26.159 --> 00:09:31.440
and if I hover over that icon, it reads, this answer was not produced by a trusted calculation

00:09:31.440 --> 00:09:38.719
and does not cite trusted context. So you can think of this as like a normal response from

00:09:38.719 --> 00:09:45.919
any other coding agent. In this case, the agent is improvising code and using its own intuition

00:09:45.919 --> 00:09:48.159
in order to try to answer your questions.

00:09:49.119 --> 00:09:53.520
So if we revisit those two problems that we were originally trying to solve,

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:57.760
the model has to write the right code and the user has to know how trustworthy that code is.

00:09:58.719 --> 00:10:05.119
So in order to help the model write the right code, we provide the model with calculations

00:10:05.119 --> 00:10:10.239
and context that the data team uses already in order to produce trustworthy analysis.

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:18.640
Then depending on how effectively the agent was able to find context or code in order to

00:10:18.640 --> 00:10:25.760
solve that problem, we can signal to the user whether it used code that exists already in

00:10:25.760 --> 00:10:33.280
the organization to answer the question or whether it had to improvise its own code in order to

00:10:34.000 --> 00:10:41.200
answer that question. So each response that comes from a commons agent follows this sort of flow.

00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:44.000
Regardless of what the question is, the first thing that the agent will do

00:10:44.559 --> 00:10:50.880
is search for trusted calculations that can speak to this question. And if it finds one,

00:10:50.880 --> 00:10:56.559
it will use it and you'll get a green shield. Otherwise, the agent will poke around and

00:10:56.640 --> 00:10:59.679
if it finds one, it will use it and you'll get a green shield.

00:11:01.280 --> 00:11:06.239
Otherwise, the agent will poke around inside of the organizational context,

00:11:06.239 --> 00:11:11.200
freehand some R or Python or SQL code, and depending on whether or not it can justify

00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:15.520
its approach, it will get a little blue icon or a little yellow one.

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:21.039
I'll hand it off to Sarah here to talk a little bit more about

00:11:21.039 --> 00:11:24.719
use cases of commons agents and what it looks like to build them.

