is, you want code as the foundation basically of everything that the model does. And the reason

00:23:12.000 --> 00:23:17.760
this isn't a given is that LLMs can do things in their head, sort of. So, you can ask it to add

00:23:17.760 --> 00:23:22.160
two numbers together or do some kind of calculation. It doesn't have to write code to do that.

00:23:22.160 --> 00:23:27.439
It can do the calculation in its head. Then that means that that calculation is not accessible to

00:23:27.439 --> 00:23:33.359
you. It's not transparent. You can't take a look at it. You can't rerun it. And also, it's often worse.

00:23:34.000 --> 00:23:38.479
And part of the reason is that LLMs are really good at writing code. This is one of the things

00:23:38.479 --> 00:23:43.920
that they really excel at. And we want to take advantage of that. And then the second reason is

00:23:43.920 --> 00:23:49.359
that we know from years and years of using code to do data analysis that code is reproducible,

00:23:49.359 --> 00:23:53.680
it's transparent, and it's auditable. And we don't want to throw all that away just because

00:23:53.680 --> 00:23:59.680
you have this shiny new LLM tool. So, as much as possible, we want the model to be able to write

00:23:59.680 --> 00:24:05.520
code. And so, the Commons agent runs its trusted calculation, which is code that you as the data

00:24:05.520 --> 00:24:11.760
scientists have vetted, but also when it writes custom code. It's not, you know, to the greatest

00:24:11.760 --> 00:24:17.199
extent possible, it's not doing those calculations in its head. It's writing code to get an answer.

00:24:17.199 --> 00:24:21.280
It shows you the code if you want to see it, and then it interprets it.

00:24:23.359 --> 00:24:30.560
We get a lot of questions about the difference between these various AI tools that Posit makes,

00:24:31.520 --> 00:24:38.719
Posit Assistant, Commons, ShinyChat, QueryChat. So, Posit Assistant is really if you are writing

00:24:38.719 --> 00:24:43.839
code, you want to be close to the code when getting help with coding or data analysis,

00:24:43.839 --> 00:24:48.319
and you want to do that in your development environment. So, Posit Assistant might help

00:24:48.319 --> 00:24:54.719
you build a Commons agent, a ShinyChat app, or a QueryChat app. And then Commons, again,

00:24:54.719 --> 00:25:02.560
I think the differentiator is really this trusted code idea. And this is when you want to build a

00:25:02.560 --> 00:25:08.640
data analysis agent for your colleagues to use, and you have some trusted code, in particular,

00:25:08.640 --> 00:25:15.680
data sources that you want the agent to use. ShinyChat underpins Commons. This is if you want

00:25:15.680 --> 00:25:21.040
to build custom Shiny apps with chat components. So, if you want to get, you know, at the lower

00:25:21.040 --> 00:25:25.119
level and build something custom, you might want to use ShinyChat. And there's also QueryChat,

00:25:25.119 --> 00:25:31.920
which helps you explore tabular data through SQL-powered chat. QueryChat and Commons have a

00:25:31.920 --> 00:25:37.439
lot of similarities. A couple differentiators are that Commons, again, has this trusted

00:25:37.439 --> 00:25:44.479
calculation idea, and QueryChat also is the agent only writes SQL code by design,

00:25:44.479 --> 00:25:48.880
whereas a Commons agent might write custom R, Python, or SQL code.

00:25:51.040 --> 00:25:57.680
QueryChat also might be a bit more customizable. Thanks. We have links to both of those

00:25:58.400 --> 00:26:01.760
demos, or to the code for both the demos, as well as to Commons.

00:26:03.439 --> 00:26:07.599
Simon and I also write a newsletter every other week called the Posit.ai Newsletter,

00:26:08.160 --> 00:26:11.599
and that link goes to all the past editions on the Posit open source blog.

00:26:13.439 --> 00:26:15.439
We're looking forward to all of your questions.

